# Task 7a. The remaining exception gaps in TeaVM's library, for the errors a first course shows (D11).
# Written from the pinned JDK's observed output and the public Javadoc only (clean room, D14): every
# message below was read off small programs run on Temurin 25.0.4.1; no OpenJDK source was opened.
#   - Throwable.getSuppressed() returns an empty array, as its Javadoc promises, and addSuppressed()
#     works, so a try-with-resources whose body and close() both throw reports the body's exception with
#     close()'s suppressed. Both threw NullPointerException: the field's initializer ran only in the
#     fakeInit constructors, never in the <init> methods that really build a Throwable.
#   - ArrayList get, set and remove: "Index 5 out of bounds for length 2"; add and addAll at an index:
#     "Index: 3, Size: 2". The exception had no message at all.
#   - StringBuilder (and StringBuffer, which shares the code), for a position outside the builder, throws
#     StringIndexOutOfBoundsException with the JDK's text: charAt, deleteCharAt and setCharAt "Index 3
#     out of bounds for length 3"; every insert "Range [4, 3) out of bounds for length 3"; delete and
#     replace "Range [4, 3) ..." after cutting the end down to the length; substring and subSequence
#     "Range [2, 7) ..."; setLength(-1) "String index out of range: -1". Before: no message, a bare
#     IndexOutOfBoundsException from charAt and substring, and most inserts past the end, setCharAt at
#     the end and setLength(-1) went through silently and left the builder broken.
#   - new T[n] with n < 0 (compiler, strict mode): NegativeArraySizeException with the size as its message
#     ("-1"), instead of a Wasm trap no catch can handle. For new T[a][b] every dimension is evaluated
#     before any is checked, then they are checked left to right, as on the JDK. A size that is a
#     non-negative constant is not checked. The dependency analysis learns that creating an array can
#     throw it, as TeaVM already does for ClassCastException at a cast (in every build, like that cast
#     rule): without that a catch for it got an empty type set, and e.getMessage() in the handler trapped.
#   - An unused array creation is kept (optimizer, every build: not only strict mode, and so also in
#     compiler.wasm itself, which the patched TeaVM compiles since teavm-javac-0105). This is what lets the
#     check above run when the array is never used (int[] a = new int[-5]; alone, the textbook demo):
#     TeaVM's unused-variable elimination deleted such a new T[n] before its check could run, so the
#     escape analysis it relies on now counts every array creation as used. That also keeps an unused
#     new T[3], which costs an allocation and changes nothing a program can see. Unused array reads and
#     casts are still deleted (F1-unused-throwing-read, F1-unused-cast).
#   - System.exit (was missing, so a program calling it did not compile) is Runtime.getRuntime().exit, as
#     the Javadoc makes it, and Runtime.exit (was an empty method) calls the host import
#     teavmConsole.exit(status) on Wasm GC. The host must not return from it: runtime/runner throws a JS
#     exception, which unwinds past every Java catch and finally (TeaVM's Wasm code catches only its own
#     exception tag), as the JDK runs no finally on exit, and reports the status. As with getcharStdin
#     (teavm-0005), a program fails to instantiate without the import only if it calls exit.
# Seen, not fixed: a bad sub-range of a char[] or CharSequence argument to StringBuilder append/insert
# (the JDK: IndexOutOfBoundsException "Range [1, 5) out of bounds for length 2"); setLength growing the
# builder does not fill with '\0'; addSuppressed(this) and addSuppressed(null) are not rejected;
# Runtime.halt and shutdown hooks do nothing.
#
# Applies to: github.com/konsoletyper/teavm tag 0.13.1 (b3a245b7), after teavm-0001 to teavm-0011. Touches
# no line an earlier patch added: WasmGCSupport, WasmGCDependencies, WasmGCGenerationContext and
# WasmGCGenerationVisitor gain their own methods away from teavm-0009's hunks (VariableEscapeAnalyzer is
# touched by no earlier patch), and the visitor avoids
# teavm-0009's CachedExpression import; DependencyGraphBuilder gains its overrides away from teavm-0001's
# hunk and, like the cast check it sits beside, does not ask for strict mode (teavm-0001 adds that
# question), so the patch applies and compiles in every leave-one-out build, which leaves out one patch and
# the patches that declare they build on it.

